   "id": "derangements",
   "title": "Derangements",
   "priority": "P2",
   "order": 12,
   "unit": "Counting with constraints",
   "quickIdea": "A permutation with no fixed point. It is an inclusion-exclusion count, not a simple subtraction.",
   "remember": [
    "!n = n! (1 - 1/1! + 1/2! - 1/3! + ... + (-1)^n/n!).",
    "Recurrence: !n = (n-1)(!(n-1) + !(n-2)), with !1 = 0, !2 = 1.",
    "Values: !3 = 2, !4 = 9, !5 = 44, !6 = 265.",
    "!n is the nearest integer to n!/e.",
    "Repeated letters break the formula - do a case analysis instead."
   ],
   "example": {
    "prompt": "Permutations of LILAC with no letter in its original position, L's indistinguishable.",
    "work": "Both L's must move into two of positions {2,4,5}: 3 choices; then one of I, A, C keeps a restriction and the count is 3! - 2! = 4.",
    "answer": "3 x 4 = 12"
   },
   "gateAngle": "GATE CS 2020 Q.42 (answer 12) tests exactly the repeated-letter case, where dividing !5 by 2! gives 22 and is wrong.",
   "variations": [
    {
     "label": "ORIGINAL PYQ-STYLE QUESTION",
     "question": "Five letters are put into five addressed envelopes at random. How many ways put every letter in the wrong envelope?",
     "answer": "44",
     "why": "That is !5 = 44. If the question instead said 'no letter in its own envelope and letter 1 in envelope 2', you would condition on one placement first - the pattern changes, not just the number."
    }
   ],
