   "id": "functional-completeness",
   "title": "Functional Completeness of Connectives",
   "priority": "P2",
   "order": 25,
   "unit": "Connectives",
   "quickIdea": "A set of connectives is functionally complete when every truth function can be built from it. NAND alone and NOR alone are complete; {and, or} is not, because it cannot express negation.",
   "remember": [
    "A set S of connectives is functionally complete when every Boolean function is expressible as a formula using only connectives from S.",
    "{not, and}, {not, or}, {not, implies}, {NAND} and {NOR} are each functionally complete.",
    "{and, or} and {and, or, implies, iff} are not complete: every formula they build is monotone, so negation is out of reach.",
    "{xor} and {xor, and} are not complete: every formula they build is 0-preserving, so negation is again out of reach.",
    "NAND builds negation as p NAND p, and then AND as not (p NAND q). NOR builds negation as p NOR p, and OR as not (p NOR q).",
    "Post's criterion: a single connective is complete only if it is not 0-preserving, not 1-preserving, not monotone, not self-dual and not affine."
   ],
   "example": {
    "prompt": "Build not p and p and q using only NAND.",
    "work": "p NAND p is not (p and p) = not p. Then p NAND q is not (p and q), so (p NAND q) NAND (p NAND q) = not not (p and q) = p and q.",
    "answer": "not p = p NAND p; p and q = (p NAND q) NAND (p NAND q)"
   },
   "gateAngle": "GATE 2015 Set 1 Q.39 gives f(X,Y,Z) = X'YZ + XY' + Y'Z' and g(X,Y,Z) = X'YZ + X'YZ' + XY and asks which singleton is complete. Setting Y = X in f gives f(X,X,Z) = X'Z', which is NOR, so {f} is complete; g preserves 0 and 1, so it cannot be.",
   "variations": [
    {
     "label": "ORIGINAL PYQ-STYLE QUESTION",
     "question": "Is {xor, and} functionally complete?",
     "answer": "No",
     "why": "Both connectives return false when every input is false, so every formula built from them is 0-preserving: at all-false input the value is false. Negation must map false to true, so it cannot be expressed."
    },
    {
     "label": "CONCEPTUAL PRACTICE QUESTION",
     "question": "Why is {and, or} not functionally complete?",
     "answer": "Every formula built from and and or is monotone, so negation cannot be expressed",
     "why": "Raising a variable from false to true can never turn a monotone formula from true to false. Negation does exactly that, so it lies outside the set."
    }
   ],
   "trap": {
    "what": "Assuming any set of connectives is complete, or checking completeness by trying a few examples.",
    "why": "Completeness is a claim about all Boolean functions. Either exhibit a known complete set (such as NAND) built from the candidate, or use an invariant such as 0-preservation that rules it out.",
    "counterexample": "{and, or} looks rich - it has two connectives and builds all monotone functions - yet not p is not monotone, so the set fails."
   },
   "check": {
    "question": "Express not p using NOR only.",
    "answer": "p NOR p",
    "explanation": "p NOR p is not (p or p) = not p. NOR is complete, so everything else can then be built from it."
   },
